      "text": "Newton's 3 Laws of Motion 🚀\n\nNewton's Laws of Motion explain why objects move, stop, speed up, slow down, or change direction.\n\nThey were developed by Isaac Newton and are the foundation of classical physics.\n\nWe'll learn each law with simple explanations, real-life examples, and a quick quiz at the end!\n\n1. Newton's First Law — The Law of Inertia\n\nAn object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion at a constant velocity, unless acted upon by a net external force.\n\nIn simpler words:\n\nA soccer ball won't move until you kick it.\n\nA moving ball will keep moving unless something slows it down or changes its direction.\n\nFriction and air resistance are forces that can slow things down.\n\nReal-life example: When a car suddenly stops, your body tends to keep moving forward. That's why seatbelts are important!\n\nRemember\n\nInertia means an object's resistance to changes in its velocity. More mass means more inertia.\n2. Newton's Second Law — Force = Mass × Acceleration\n\nThis law explains how forces change an object's motion.\n\nnzherald.co.nz\nF = ma\n\nNet Force = Mass × Acceleration\n\nHere's what the letters mean:\n\nF = Net force, measured in newtons (N)\n\nm = Mass, measured in kilograms (kg)\n\na = Acceleration, measured in m/s²\n\nExample: Imagine pushing an empty shopping cart versus a full one. The full cart requires more force to accelerate at the same rate because it has more mass.\n\nTry changing the force and mass\n\nMass\n\n5 kg\n\nNet force\n\n20 N\n\nResulting acceleration\n\n4.00 m/s²\n\n20 ÷ 5 = 4.00\n\nTry increasing the mass while keeping the force the same. What happens to acceleration?\n\nKey idea: More net force means more acceleration. More mass means less acceleration for the same net force.\n\n3. Newton's Third Law — Action and Reaction\n\nFor every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.\n\nlaunchphotography.com\n\nImagine a rocket launching into space.\n\nThe rocket pushes exhaust gases downward.\n\nThe gases push the rocket upward with an equal and opposite force.\n\nThis helps the rocket accelerate upward.\n\nAnother example is jumping. Your feet push the ground downward, and the ground pushes you upward.\n\nImportant: The two forces act on different objects, so they don't cancel each other out on one object.\n\nQuick summary\n\nLaw\n\n\t\n\nMain idea\n\n\t\n\nExample\n\n\n\n\n1st — Inertia\n\n\t\n\nMotion doesn't change without net force\n\n\t\n\nA ball sitting still\n\n\n\n\n2nd — F = ma\n\n\t\n\nNet force causes acceleration\n\n\t\n\nPushing a cart\n\n\n\n\n3rd — Action–Reaction\n\n\t\n\nForces come in equal, opposite pairs\n\n\t\n\nA rocket launching\n\nTest your knowledge! 🧠\n\nQUESTION 1 OF 3\n\nA book stays on a table until someone pushes it. Which law explains this?\n\nFirst Law\nSecond Law\nThird Law\n\nQUESTION 2 OF 3\n\nA 4 kg object accelerates at 3 m/s². What is the net force?\n\n7 N\n12 N\n1.33 N\n\nQUESTION 3 OF 3\n\nWhen you swim, you push water backward and the water pushes you forward. Which law?\n\nFirst Law\nSecond Law\nThird Law\n\nThe easiest way to remember Newton's laws is:\n\n1st Law: Objects resist changes in motion.\n\n2nd Law: Forces cause acceleration.\n\n3rd Law: Forces always come in pairs.\n\nOnce you understand these three ideas, you can explain everything from kicking a soccer ball to launching a spacecraft!",
