      "text": "Yes! Newton's three laws of motion apply in space too! 🚀 In fact, space is one of the best places to understand them because there's very little air resistance or friction.\n\n1. First Law — Inertia in Space\n\nImagine an astronaut floating in space who throws a ball.\n\nThe ball keeps moving in a straight line at constant speed if no net force acts on it.\n\nUnlike on Earth, there's almost no air resistance to slow it down.\n\nHowever, gravity from nearby planets, stars, and other objects can change its motion.\n\nFun fact: A spacecraft can coast through space without continuously firing its engines!\n\n2. Second Law — F = ma in Space\nspace.com\n\nSuppose an astronaut pushes a 10 kg object with a net force of 20 N.\n\nUsing Newton's second law:\n\n𝑎\n=\n𝐹\n𝑚\n=\n20\n10\n=\n2\n m/s\n2\na=\nm\nF\n\t​\n\n=\n10\n20\n\t​\n\n=2 m/s\n2\n\nThe object accelerates at 2 m/s², just as it would on Earth under the same net force.\n\n3. Third Law — Rockets Work in Space\nspacevoyageventures.com\n\nA common misconception is that rockets need air to push against.\n\nThey don't!\n\nThe rocket pushes exhaust gases backward.\n\nThe exhaust gases push the rocket forward.\n\nThis works even in the vacuum of space!\n\nThat's Newton's third law.\n\nBut there's one important detail!\n\nNewton's laws are extremely accurate for ordinary spacecraft motion, but they have limitations.\n\nAt speeds close to the speed of light, we need Einstein's special relativity. For extremely strong gravity or very precise calculations, we may need general relativity.\n\nQuick question for you 🧠\n\nIf an astronaut throws a ball in deep space, far from significant gravitational influences, what will happen to it?\n\nA. It will gradually slow down and stop.\nB. It will continue moving at constant velocity.\nC. It will accelerate forever.",
