      "state": "state_0",
      "statesRecorded": 1,
      "text": "Exactly! 🎯 You've understood Newton's First Law!\n\nAn object in motion will keep moving forever at the same speed and in the same direction, as long as no net external force acts on it.\n\nThis happens because of inertia.\n\n🚀 Imagine this experiment in space\n\nYou're an astronaut floating in deep space, far away from planets and stars.\n\nYou throw a ball at 10 m/s.\n\nAssuming no external forces act on it:\n\nTime after throwing\n\n\t\n\nSpeed\n\n\n\n\n1 second\n\n\t\n\n10 m/s\n\n\n\n\n1 minute\n\n\t\n\n10 m/s\n\n\n\n\n1 hour\n\n\t\n\n10 m/s\n\n\n\n\n1 year\n\n\t\n\n10 m/s\n\n\n\n\n1 million years\n\n\t\n\n10 m/s\n\nThe ball would continue moving at 10 m/s indefinitely!\n\nBut why do objects stop on Earth?\n\nImagine rolling a ball across the floor.\n\nIt eventually stops because of:\n\nFriction between the ball and the floor.\n\nAir resistance opposing its motion.\n\nWithout these forces (or any other net force), the ball would continue moving.\n\nHere's something interesting! 🧠\n\nInertia doesn't actually push an object forward.\n\nAn object doesn't need a force to keep moving. It only needs a net force to change its velocity.\n\nThat's one of Newton's most important discoveries.\n\nLet me challenge you with a question!\n\nIf a spaceship is traveling through deep space at 20,000 km/h and suddenly turns off its engines, what happens?\n\nA. It immediately stops.\nB. It gradually slows down.\nC. It keeps moving at 20,000 km/h.",
